ause  it gives you access whether it's an  access to do a deal and access to get  insight into what's going on inside a  government and i would go out and i talk  to people i talk to 150 people in doing  this book and time after time after time  when i talk to people sort of within the  heart of the super class one of the  first things they said is small world  and their you know the small world  phenomenon came up over and over i can  get to anybody with a phone call i can  get to anybody with two phone calls i  can get use that as insight that i can  trade upon or insight that i ca or  connections that i can use from the  point of view of influence and so forth  and so  that  you know is is another thing that one  can pick up from from studying a group  like this  some some dimensions of this  are  are are are striking in that they really  force us  to step away from our preconceptions all  of us have been raised and sort of  thinking in terms of  well you know the treaty of west failure  i know most of you right now i can look  at you and see that you're thinking of  the treaty of west failure um it you  know almost never leaves the forefront  of my mind but you know the notion that  there was this treaty 400 years ago and  we started from then on thinking of the  way the world is organized in terms of  chunks of political entities known as  nation states but the role of those  nation states in this particular era has  been changed fairly dramatically and  this i mean we people have been noting  this for some time jessica matthews  wrote a paper  over a decade ago called powershift  which was a very important  uh early acknowledgement of what was  happening here but that power shift has  taken place in ways that are really  really interesting and i think  haven't been taken into consideration by  policymakers in a lot of respects for  one hand the concentration of power in  the hands of fewer and fewer people has  grown and grown and grown  in the 50s when c wrig